  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =3y \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}.$",
